WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.596 --> 00:00:20.377
Marius was an attorney, he was not interested in practicing law.

2
00:00:21.197 --> 00:00:24.498
It was tiring to think of dealing with lawyers and attending court.

3
00:00:25.439 --> 00:00:29.400
Of course, he could probably make more money as a lawyer than as a translator.

4
00:00:30.284 --> 00:00:35.108
But the publishing house provided him with steady work that left him with enough free time to do

5
00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:35.889
as he pleased.

6
00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:42.994
Marius delighted in taking long walks along the outer boulevards of Paris or on the quiet paths of

7
00:00:43.034 --> 00:00:45.196
the Luxembourg Gardens, a large park.

8
00:00:46.097 --> 00:00:49.760
He sometimes spent half a day doing nothing but admiring the gardens.

9
00:00:50.841 --> 00:00:54.544
It was during one of these walks that he discovered the old Gorbeau house.

10
00:00:55.565 --> 00:00:59.388
Its isolation and cheapness attracted him, so he rented a room there.

11
00:01:00.564 --> 00:01:05.387
Marius spent so much time outdoors that he was barely aware that he had neighbors.

12
00:01:07.248 --> 00:01:15.693
One day in the summer of 1831, the housekeeper mentioned that Marius'neighbors, the wretched Jondrette family, were about

13
00:01:15.753 --> 00:01:17.294
to be thrown out into the street.

14
00:01:17.314 --> 00:01:19.155
"Why is that?"

15
00:01:19.575 --> 00:01:20.355
he asked the woman.

16
00:01:21.336 --> 00:01:23.417
"Because they don't pay their rent.

17
00:01:23.997 --> 00:01:25.999
They're behind by at least several months."

18
00:01:27.039 --> 00:01:28.200
"How much does that amount to?"

19
00:01:28.852 --> 00:01:31.134
"Twenty francs," she replied.

20
00:01:32.055 --> 00:01:34.677
Marius had thirty francs in reserve in a drawer.

21
00:01:35.277 --> 00:01:39.861
He didn't know these people at all, but at this point even he had more money than they

22
00:01:39.881 --> 00:01:46.166
did. Marius was quite a kind person, so he said, "I'll give you 25 francs.

23
00:01:46.706 --> 00:01:50.089
Take out twenty for the rent and give the extra five to the Jondrettes.

24
00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:52.831
But don't tell them that the money came from me."

25
00:01:53.952 --> 00:01:58.516
Marius was now a fine-looking young man of medium height with jet-black hair.

26
00:01:59.432 --> 00:02:03.894
At the time of his worst poverty, he noticed girls looking at him when he passed.

27
00:02:05.555 --> 00:02:09.896
He could feel them staring and he guessed that they were laughing at his old clothes.

28
00:02:10.877 --> 00:02:14.598
But they were really staring after him because he was so handsome.

29
00:02:15.759 --> 00:02:21.341
As Marius walked through the Luxembourg Gardens, he always saw the same couple sitting on the same bench:

30
00:02:22.302 --> 00:02:27.604
an older man with white hair and a girl whom Marius assumed was the man's daughter.

31
00:02:28.584 --> 00:02:35.068
Marius began thinking of the man as Monsieur Leblanc because he had white hair, and blanc means "white"

32
00:02:35.129 --> 00:02:35.629
in French.

33
00:02:36.529 --> 00:02:38.131
The girl was not very pretty.

34
00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:44.375
Thin and awkward, almost ugly, she wore an ill-fitting dress of black wool and a velvet hat.

35
00:02:45.415 --> 00:02:49.038
Then Marius did not walk in the park for about six months.

36
00:02:50.119 --> 00:02:56.343
When he returned, Monsieur Leblanc was still sitting on the same bench, but now the girl next to

37
00:02:56.403 --> 00:02:57.844
him was truly beautiful.

38
00:02:58.736 --> 00:03:02.038
At first Marius thought she must be the original girl's sister.

39
00:03:03.279 --> 00:03:08.103
But he soon realized that this lovely creature with the brown hair and blue eyes was the same

40
00:03:08.203 --> 00:03:13.466
girl. One day Marius walked past her and their eyes met.

41
00:03:14.007 --> 00:03:18.130
The next morning he put on his new coat, trousers, hat, and boots.

42
00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.872
In the park he instantly spotted the girl and her father.

43
00:03:22.553 --> 00:03:27.016
Marius did not dare to look at her as he passed, but he blushed deep red.

44
00:03:27.956 --> 00:03:32.899
The young man had fallen desperately in love, but he didn't want to be too obvious.

45
00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:38.302
He didn't parade in front of her anymore because he didn't want to attract her father's attention.

46
00:03:39.143 --> 00:03:44.046
Instead he went to the park and sat on a bench within sight of his new love.

47
00:03:45.086 --> 00:03:48.088
Meanwhile the girl had also noticed Marius.

48
00:03:48.889 --> 00:03:53.371
She turned on her bench, so that she could listen to her father and look at Marius at

49
00:03:53.411 --> 00:03:54.112
the same time.

50
00:03:55.152 --> 00:03:58.613
Sometimes her father got up and walked with the girl to another bench.

51
00:03:59.253 --> 00:04:00.414
Marius followed them.

52
00:04:01.394 --> 00:04:04.335
Sometimes the father began to come without the daughter.

53
00:04:05.755 --> 00:04:08.356
On those days Marius left the park early.

54
00:04:09.396 --> 00:04:14.397
One night Marius found a handkerchief on the bench where the father and daughter had just been sitting.

55
00:04:15.018 --> 00:04:17.678
It was marked with the initials U.F.

56
00:04:17.698 --> 00:04:21.619
"Her name must be Ursula," he said to himself.

57
00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:23.380
"What a sweet name!"

58
00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:28.103
At first it was enough for Marius to believe that he knew the girl's name.

59
00:04:28.664 --> 00:04:30.966
He could live on that fact alone for weeks.

60
00:04:32.127 --> 00:04:35.009
But after a month, he wanted to know more about her.

61
00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:37.111
He wanted to know where she lived.

62
00:04:38.032 --> 00:04:42.015
He had already made one mistake by following the girl when she moved to another bench.

63
00:04:43.096 --> 00:04:47.140
Then he did not stay in the park when Monsieur Leblanc came without the girl.

64
00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:52.044
Now he made a third error: He followed Ursula home.

65
00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:58.313
She lived in the Rue de l'Ouest, in the least frequented part of the street, in a house

66
00:04:58.353 --> 00:04:59.394
of modest appearance.

67
00:05:00.194 --> 00:05:03.935
But this piece of knowledge only increased Marius'need for more information.

68
00:05:05.415 --> 00:05:11.897
One night after he had followed Ursula and Monsieur Leblanc home, Marius entered the building and talked to

69
00:05:11.937 --> 00:05:12.357
the porter.

70
00:05:12.377 --> 00:05:16.758
"Is that gentleman who just came in the one who lives on the second floor?"

71
00:05:17.259 --> 00:05:19.299
asked Marius, fishing for information.

72
00:05:19.319 --> 00:05:22.240
"No, he lives on the fourth floor."

73
00:05:23.132 --> 00:05:24.392
Another fact for Marius.

74
00:05:25.133 --> 00:05:28.774
His success made him bolder, and he dared to ask another question.

75
00:05:28.794 --> 00:05:32.375
"In the apartment with the windows facing the street?"

76
00:05:32.395 --> 00:05:36.196
"All the apartments face the street," replied the porter.

77
00:05:36.216 --> 00:05:39.577
"And what is this gentleman's occupation?"

78
00:05:40.637 --> 00:05:43.078
"He lives on the income from his investments, monsieur.

79
00:05:43.938 --> 00:05:48.479
He's a very kind man, who does a great deal of good among the poor even though he's

80
00:05:48.539 --> 00:05:49.039
not rich."

81
00:05:49.999 --> 00:05:50.640
"What's his name?"

82
00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:51.660
asked Marius.

83
00:05:52.392 --> 00:05:54.493
The porter studied Marius for a moment.

84
00:05:55.953 --> 00:05:57.594
"Are you a detective?"

85
00:05:57.614 --> 00:05:59.694
"Oh! No."

86
00:06:00.174 --> 00:06:03.735
Marius was too flustered to say anything more and left the building.

87
00:06:04.656 --> 00:06:08.437
But he was still in high spirits because he was making progress.

88
00:06:09.597 --> 00:06:14.518
The next day Monsieur Leblanc and his daughter made only a short visit to the Luxembourg Gardens.

89
00:06:15.319 --> 00:06:17.799
Marius followed them back to the Rue de l'Ouest.

90
00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:22.834
Upon reaching the door, Monsieur Leblanc let his daughter enter ahead of him.

91
00:06:23.454 --> 00:06:27.757
Then he turned around and looked steadily at Marius.

92
00:06:28.978 --> 00:06:30.099
Marius hurried away.

93
00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:34.542
To his dismay, they did not come to the park at all the next day.

94
00:06:35.203 --> 00:06:37.545
Marius waited in vain all afternoon.

95
00:06:38.665 --> 00:06:43.449
At nightfall he went to the Rue de l'Ouest and saw a light in the fourth-floor windows.

96
00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:47.572
He walked beneath these windows until the light was put out.

97
00:06:48.192 --> 00:06:50.233
He did not mind that he had missed dinner.

98
00:06:52.014 --> 00:06:53.935
Marius passed a week in this manner.

99
00:06:54.835 --> 00:07:00.697
Monsieur Leblanc and his daughter did not reappear in the park, but Marius did not dare to watch

100
00:07:00.737 --> 00:07:02.078
their building during the day.

101
00:07:03.178 --> 00:07:08.821
Sometimes at night he saw shadows moving against the blinds, and his heart beat quickly.

102
00:07:09.621 --> 00:07:12.682
On the eighth day there was no light in the windows.

103
00:07:12.702 --> 00:07:16.084
"Perhaps they've gone out," Marius said to himself.

104
00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:22.544
He waited until one o'clock in the morning, but no one entered the building and no light appeared

105
00:07:22.564 --> 00:07:23.224
at the windows.

106
00:07:24.025 --> 00:07:28.328
There was no light again the next night, so Marius knocked on the front door.

107
00:07:28.348 --> 00:07:31.890
"Has the gentleman on the fourth floor gone on vacation?"

108
00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:33.111
he asked the porter.

109
00:07:33.131 --> 00:07:36.333
"No, he moved out yesterday."

110
00:07:37.114 --> 00:07:42.077
Marius tottered, but managed to say weakly, "Where does he live now?"

111
00:07:42.097 --> 00:07:46.040
"I have no idea," said the porter, shutting the door.

112
00:07:46.676 --> 00:07:48.419
"He left no forwarding address."
